      "writtenOn": "2026-10-21T08:45:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "How is the handler meant to do flow control here? append() never reports how much is queued and nothing signals when the reader drains, so the handler can only cap total size, which leaves streaming with the same memory profile as aggregation. Would a queued-bytes count plus a low-water callback (fired outside the lock) make sense, or should the Javadoc say only a size limit is expected?",
      "revId": "972773dab40b671dd6242817386e1d63639a3a87",
      "serverId": "7fc14799-209e-464c-9743-7a06c2c21a81"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7f39bc7e_e69ba858",
        "filename": "transport/transport-http/src/main/java/org/opendaylight/netconf/transport/http/ServerRequestExecutor.java",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 124,
